                    coach_default:          `Call committed.`,
                    coach_r1_acquire:       `<strong>Acquisition.</strong> You own the IP and the talent now, and you ship before the cliff — the €16M of ARR is safe. But you've never integrated a foreign, research-led culture, and the value you're buying walks out of the building every night. What does ownership buy you that an alliance doesn't — and is it worth €70M more and the integration risk? The next two rounds decide whether this was bold or value-destroying.`,
                    coach_r1_alliance:      `<strong>Alliance.</strong> You get most of the capability for a quarter of the cash, you ship before the cliff, and it's reversible — strong strategic agility. The cost: you neither own the IP nor control the roadmap, and the partner could later be bought by the rival. Watch that exposure in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r1_build:         `<strong>Organic build.</strong> Full control, no culture clash — and a 20–24-month time-to-ship. Your three at-risk customers, €16M of ARR, renew <em>before</em> that. You'll have a beautiful module and no one to sell it to, because the rival took them. Speed to the cliff was the whole point. Does build survive that constraint?`,
                    coach_r1_build_contradiction: `<strong>You named speed as the deciding factor — then chose to build.</strong> Build ships in 20–24 months; the cliff is ~12. If speed truly decides it, build is the one mode that fails the test. Mode choice is dominated by time-to-value when the cliff is real.`,
                    coach_r1_boldness:      `<strong>You're acquiring because it's the boldest, most decisive move.</strong> Maybe. But an alliance gets you most of the capability for a quarter of the cash and is reversible. The boldest mode is not automatically the value-maximizing one — decisiveness is not a deal thesis.`,
